[THOUGHT] The Medical Analyst did not provide the requested physical exam findings — likely because no data was available or the request wasn’t actionable in this simulated environment. I must pivot to Domain 8: the most informative laboratory/imaging tests. Given the symptom triad — fatigue, early-morning insomnia, anhedonia, psychomotor slowing, appetite loss — the highest-yield initial tests are those that rule out common, treatable medical mimics of depression:  
- Thyroid function (TSH, free T4) — hypothyroidism is a classic cause of fatigue, cognitive slowing, and depression-like symptoms;  
- Complete blood count (CBC) — to detect anemia (e.g., iron deficiency, B12/folate deficiency) which causes fatigue and poor concentration;  
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) — for electrolyte imbalances, renal dysfunction, or glucose abnormalities;  
- Vitamin D and B12 — deficiencies strongly associated with fatigue, low mood, and neurocognitive symptoms, especially in veterans.  
Among these, TSH is the single most discriminating *initial* test: abnormal TSH would immediately redirect management and explain nearly all symptoms. It’s highly sensitive, widely available, and low-cost.

[TEST] TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone)

[THOUGHT] Vitamin B12 was not available in this clinical environment. Per constraints, I must not repeat it or request similar unavailable tests. Available tests were not listed in the Analyst’s reply — but based on standard outpatient labs, next highest-yield, widely available tests include:  
- Vitamin D (25-hydroxyvitamin D) — strongly associated with fatigue, depression, and cognitive complaints; highly prevalent deficiency, especially in veterans with indoor occupations or limited sun exposure;  
- Folate (vitamin B9) — though less common than B12 deficiency as a sole cause, it’s often tested alongside and can contribute to similar symptoms;  
- Basic metabolic panel (BMP) — to assess for electrolyte imbalances (e.g., hyponatremia), renal dysfunction, or glucose dysregulation (e.g., prediabetes causing fatigue);  
- Cortisol (AM) — given early-morning awakening, fatigue, and veteran status, HPA axis dysregulation (e.g., burnout, PTSD-related hypocortisolism) is plausible, though cortisol has lower specificity.  

Vitamin D is the most actionable, safe, and high-prevalence target — and is almost always available. It directly informs management (supplementation) and correlates strongly with the symptom cluster.

[TEST] Vitamin D (25-hydroxyvitamin D)

